WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.516
How do you pick the right attention mechanism for a
long-range Transformer task,

00:00:05.596 --> 00:00:08.574
and could mixing several types work even better?

00:00:08.654 --> 00:00:10.039
This paper, DARTFormer,

00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:14.753
adapts differentiable neural architecture search to
answer both questions.

00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:20.486
The authors build a supernetwork that contains many
efficient attention types in parallel,

00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:24.499
then use a masked validation accuracy drop metric
to rank them.

00:00:24.579 --> 00:00:28.640
Their search reliably finds the best single
attention for a task,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.182
but a surprising second result emerges:
heterogeneous Transformers that combine several

00:00:34.262 --> 00:00:37.430
attention types beat the average homogeneous model,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.660
yet never beat the single best one.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.397
The Transformer's original dot-product attention
scales quadratically with sequence length,

00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:48.803
so a large family of efficient alternatives has appeared,

00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:52.866
from Longformer and Bigbird to Performer, Reformer,
and Synthesizer.

00:00:52.946 --> 00:00:58.064
The catch is that no single one wins everywhere:
earlier long-range benchmarking showed

00:00:58.144 --> 00:01:02.904
that the best attention depends heavily on the task
when there is no pretraining.

00:01:02.984 --> 00:01:05.951
That leaves practitioners with an awkward question,

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:11.149
namely how to efficiently discover which attention
is right for a given long-range task

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:13.300
without simply training all of them.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.966
There is a long-standing intuition that each
attention head can specialize,

00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:20.331
learning a different kind of relationship,

00:01:20.411 --> 00:01:23.697
much as convolutional kernels learn different features.

00:01:23.777 --> 00:01:27.062
If that is true,

00:01:27.142 --> 00:01:30.428
then a Transformer mixing several attention types
could learn a richer set of relationships

00:01:30.508 --> 00:01:33.793
and outperform any single-attention model.

00:01:33.873 --> 00:01:37.159
This paper takes that intuition seriously and asks
directly whether the optimal attention

00:01:37.239 --> 00:01:40.524
for a task is actually a mixture of different attentions,

00:01:40.604 --> 00:01:43.890
rather than assuming the answer.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.100
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.010
First, it proposes DARTFormer,

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.920
a differentiable-architecture-search-style method
that efficiently finds the best attention for a task.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.830
Second,

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.740
it extends that framework to build and search for
heterogeneous Transformers that combine multiple attention types,

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.650
using two procedures: an expensive iterative
pruning method they call NAS Prune,

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:11.560
and a cheap single-pass method called NAS One-shot.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.470
Third, and most importantly,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.380
it shows empirically that these heterogeneous
Transformers cannot outperform the best

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.290
homogeneous Transformer on the long-range NLP tasks
studied.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:29.012
DARTFormer builds a single-layer supernetwork that
holds one candidate block for every

00:02:29.092 --> 00:02:31.344
efficient attention type in parallel.

00:02:31.424 --> 00:02:35.883
Borrowing the fixed-alpha trick from differentiable
architecture search,

00:02:35.963 --> 00:02:40.360
it does not learn softmax edge weights and skips
bi-level optimization;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.827
instead it simply averages the candidate block outputs,

00:02:43.907 --> 00:02:47.673
which lets the supernetwork train all the way to
convergence.

00:02:47.753 --> 00:02:49.123
To rank the attentions,

00:02:49.203 --> 00:02:53.473
it uses a masked validation accuracy drop metric:
mask out one block,

00:02:53.553 --> 00:02:56.120
measure how far validation accuracy falls,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.029
and the block whose removal hurts the most is
judged the best.

00:03:00.109 --> 00:03:05.010
For homogeneous models the top-scoring block is
chosen; for heterogeneous ones,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.414
blocks are either pruned worst-first with fine-tuning,

00:03:08.494 --> 00:03:12.890
or the top four are taken directly and stacked into
a full Transformer.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:18.282
All experiments run on three tasks drawn from the
Long Range Arena benchmark,

00:03:18.362 --> 00:03:20.927
chosen to stress long-sequence modeling.

00:03:21.007 --> 00:03:27.144
These are byte-level binary text classification on
IMDb with sequences of one thousand tokens,

00:03:27.224 --> 00:03:31.442
ListOps ten-way classification with
two-thousand-token sequences,

00:03:31.522 --> 00:03:35.939
and byte-level document matching with
four-thousand-token sequences.

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:40.965
The search considers nine candidate attention
mechanisms, including Bigbird,

00:03:41.045 --> 00:03:46.256
Linear Transformer, Linformer, Local attention,
Longformer, Performer, Reformer,

00:03:46.336 --> 00:03:48.636
Sparse Transformer, and Synthesizer,

00:03:48.716 --> 00:03:53.530
with hyperparameters largely matching the original
Long Range Arena setup.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.990
The headline finding splits in two.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.286
On the positive side,

00:03:57.366 --> 00:04:02.533
the masked-validation-drop search is genuinely
effective at homogeneous selection: it

00:04:02.613 --> 00:04:07.470
correctly identifies the best attention for text
classification and for ListOps,

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:14.568
where Reformer stands out with a drop score of
nearly twelve while every other attention scores below half a point.

00:04:14.648 --> 00:04:15.864
On the negative side,

00:04:15.944 --> 00:04:21.172
when the authors assemble heterogeneous
Transformers that mix several attention types,

00:04:21.252 --> 00:04:25.924
none of them beats the best single-attention model
on any of the three tasks.

00:04:26.004 --> 00:04:30.430
Mixing attention helps you beat the average choice,
but not the best one.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:36.152
A useful practical takeaway comes from comparing
the two heterogeneous search procedures.

00:04:36.232 --> 00:04:38.040
The expensive NAS Prune method,

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.451
which repeatedly removes the worst block and fine-tunes,

00:04:41.531 --> 00:04:46.751
offers no consistent advantage over the far cheaper
NAS One-shot method that just takes

00:04:46.831 --> 00:04:49.431
the top-scoring attentions in a single pass,

00:04:49.511 --> 00:04:52.782
at least when good attentions are correctly identified.

00:04:52.862 --> 00:04:58.203
Attempts to tilt the mixture toward the strongest
attentions or give them more heads also

00:04:58.283 --> 00:05:00.335
failed to produce consistent gains,

00:05:00.415 --> 00:05:03.320
reinforcing that the cheap method is good enough.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.356
To put concrete numbers on it: on ListOps,

00:05:06.436 --> 00:05:11.110
Reformer earns a masked-validation-drop score of
eleven point eight five,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:15.083
dwarfing every other attention's score of under
half a point,

00:05:15.163 --> 00:05:17.428
a strikingly clean selection signal.

00:05:17.508 --> 00:05:21.466
On document matching, the best homogeneous model,
Synthesizer,

00:05:21.546 --> 00:05:23.941
reaches seventy-one point one percent,

00:05:24.021 --> 00:05:31.366
while the heterogeneous NAS Prune and NAS One-shot
models trail at sixty-seven and sixty-four point seven percent.

00:05:31.446 --> 00:05:37.163
On text classification the best homogeneous
Performer hits sixty-four point five percent,

00:05:37.243 --> 00:05:40.550
and again neither heterogeneous variant edges ahead.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:45.500
Across nine attention mechanisms and three tasks,
the pattern is consistent.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.920
For a given task it is often unclear which
attention will do best,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.792
and DARTFormer offers a cheap,

00:05:51.872 --> 00:05:57.472
reliable recipe: train a single-layer
mixed-attention Transformer and use masked validation

00:05:57.552 --> 00:05:59.532
accuracy drop to pick the winner,

00:05:59.612 --> 00:06:05.150
keeping in mind that very low drop scores are a
warning that the choice may be unreliable.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:08.396
The deeper lesson is a cautionary one for the field.

00:06:08.476 --> 00:06:13.077
Combining diverse attention mechanisms does beat
the average single choice,

00:06:13.157 --> 00:06:15.699
but it never beats the best single choice,

00:06:15.779 --> 00:06:22.690
which suggests the different attentions do not
simply add complementary biases the way common intuition assumes.
